++First, you need the vulnerable program to exploit. Save this as vuln.c. It has a classic buffer overflow: it reads 100 bytes into a 64-byte buffer without checking the size.

// vuln.c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>

void vulnerable_function() {
    char buffer[64]; // Small buffer
    printf("Buffer address: %p\n", (void*)buffer);
    gets(buffer); // No bounds checking!
    printf("You said: %s\n", buffer);
}

int main() {
    vulnerable_function();
    return 0;
}

++Compile it with protections disabled so the exploit works reliably:
gcc -m32 -fno-stack-protector -z execstack -o vuln vuln.c
-m32: 32-bit architecture (simpler offset calculations).
-fno-stack-protector: Disables canaries.
-z execstack: Makes the stack executable (so we can run shellcode there).
